Legitimate research involving human subjects follows rigorous ethical frameworks overseen by independent institutional review boards (IRBs) or ethics committees. Any ethical research that collects personal online data requires:
1. Full, transparent informed consent from all participants, who must be clearly told what data will be collected, how it will be used, and how their privacy will be protected;
2. Anonymization or pseudonymization of data to prevent the identification of individual participants;
3. Minimization of data collection to only what is strictly necessary for the research question;
4. Secure storage and permanent deletion of data once the research is complete.

If you are conducting academic or scientific research, work with your institution’s ethics board to design a study that upholds the privacy and autonomy of all individuals involved. There are no legitimate "secret" methods to observe others’ online behavior for research that do not cause profound harm and violate legal and ethical standards.

